'''Group cognition''' concerns the cognitive processes that occur through interaction within a group. Participants may construct shared meanings, solve problems or maintain common representations through conversation and joint activity.

== Unit of analysis ==
Gerry Stahl's ''Group Cognition'' (2006) argues that small-group collaborative meaning-making can be studied at the interaction level. A group's sequence of utterances, diagrams and revisions can exhibit problem-solving structure not adequately described by isolated individual test scores. The approach complements individual cognitive studies rather than denying individual mechanisms.

== Empirical methods ==
Collect time-stamped conversation and artifact histories; identify episodes in which a proposal is introduced, challenged, reformulated or adopted. Define coding rules and compare analyst agreement. Examine how shared artifacts stabilize referents and how misunderstandings are repaired. For a mathematical collaboration, trace which contributions enable later derivations rather than attribute every success to the most vocal participant.

== Distinction from collective intelligence ==
Collective intelligence often evaluates group-level performance, while group cognition analyzes how group meaning and reasoning develop. A group can exhibit rich interaction yet perform poorly; an independent estimate aggregator can perform well with minimal group interaction. Distributed cognition includes wider coordination across artifacts and settings. For AI groups, trace message dependencies, reasoning artifacts and errors, but do not infer subjective collective experience from coordinated outputs.

